   plot="The title poem sets the rhetoric of national glory — 'Do not weep, maiden, for war is kind' — against the bodies of the dead, and the book's other poems present monologues of suffering, of the dying soldier, of the woman abandoned and of the universe's indifference.",
   ctx="1898-99: the Spanish-American War, the imperial rhetoric of 1898, and Crane's own experience as a war correspondent in Cuba and Greece.",
   sig="'War Is Kind' is one of the great American anti-war poems and the collection defines the irony of the 1890s: its title became a permanent phrase for the hypocrisy of martial rhetoric, and its free-verse monologues anticipate the war poetry of 1914-18.",
